{
 "lead": "Dryers remove water from a wet solid by bringing it into contact with hot, dry air. Batch dryers dry a fixed lot; continuous dryers take a steady feed and give a steady product. The choice follows the solid: slabs, pastes, slurries and granules each need a different contact pattern. In through-circulation drying, air is forced through a bed of granular solid, so the air picks up water and cools as it climbs. That humidity rise sets the drying time. The same idea sizes tray, belt and some continuous dryers in pottery, minerals, food and pharma.",
 "prereq": "You need drying rate curves and drying time from <a href=\"#drying-rate-curves-and-estimation-of-drying-time\">7.7</a> and psychrometry from <a href=\"#psychrometric-humidity-chart-and-its-use\">7.2</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Batch and continuous dryers",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Dryers are grouped by how they handle solids. Batch dryers hold a lot and dry it for a set time. Tray or compartment dryers put wet solid on trays and blow hot air over them; they suit pottery, pigments and pastes. Vacuum shelf dryers do the same under low pressure, so water boils below 100 °C; they suit heat-sensitive chemicals. Freeze dryers freeze the solid and remove ice by sublimation; they suit vaccines and instant coffee."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Continuous dryers receive wet solid and deliver dry solid without stopping. Tunnel dryers move trucks of trays through zones. Belt dryers carry a layer on a perforated belt. Rotary dryers tumble granules in a sloping cylinder with hot gas. Drum dryers dry a paste on a heated rotating drum. Spray dryers atomise a slurry into hot gas and make powder, as in milk powder. Fluidised-bed dryers suspend free-flowing particles in upward air. Pneumatic or flash dryers carry fine particles in a fast gas stream for seconds. The feed form decides the contact pattern: slabs need surface air, slurries need atomisation, granules need tumbling or fluidisation."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Through-circulation drying",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "In through-circulation drying, air is pushed through a bed of granular solid held on a perforated tray or belt. The air enters the bottom face at dry-bulb T1 and humidity Y1. As it climbs, it picks up water. At the top face its humidity is Y2 and its temperature T2, lower because evaporation takes latent heat. The bed therefore sees air that is driest and hottest at the inlet face and wettest and coolest at the outlet face. Pressure drop rises with bed depth, air velocity and fines; it must be paid for in fan power. Cross-circulation, by contrast, blows air over the tray surface, not through the bed."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The water balance on the air gives the drying rate. If m<sub>da</sub> kg dry air/h flows through and its humidity rises from Y1 to Y2 kg water/kg dry air, water picked up = m<sub>da</sub> (Y2 − Y1) kg/h. For M<sub>s</sub> kg dry solid drying from X1 to X2 kg water/kg dry solid, water to remove = M<sub>s</sub> (X1 − X2) kg."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "t = M<sub>s</sub> (X1 − X2) / [m<sub>da</sub> (Y2 − Y1)]",
     "where": "t is drying time in h; M<sub>s</sub> is dry solid mass in kg; X1 and X2 are initial and final solid moistures in kg water/kg dry solid; m<sub>da</sub> is dry-air mass flow in kg dry air/h; Y1 and Y2 are inlet and outlet air humidities in kg water/kg dry air."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "This assumes the solid surface stays wet. The air cools along its adiabatic line and can at most reach its adiabatic saturation humidity Y<sub>as</sub>, so the shortest possible time is M<sub>s</sub>(X1 − X2)/[m<sub>da</sub>(Y<sub>as</sub> − Y1)]. How close the air gets depends on the bed depth z:"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Choosing a dryer",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Selection rules follow from the solid and the scale. Heat-sensitive solids go to vacuum, freeze or low-temperature continuous dryers; high temperature ruins drugs, enzymes and some foods. Particle size matters: fine slurries are spray dried; free-flowing granules go to fluidised beds; lumps and minerals go to rotary dryers. Tonnage decides batch versus continuous: a few hundred kilograms per batch suits trays; steady output of many tonnes per hour suits continuous units. Direct heating mixes hot gas with the solid. It is simple and efficient, but the gas must be clean enough for food or pharma. Indirect heating uses a hot wall or steam tubes. It keeps combustion products away from the product and suits flammable solvents, but it is slower. A continuous unit is not automatically better; it needs steady feed and steady product quality. The exam favourite is to match feed form to dryer, not to memorise a list."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "figure": {
  "after_section": 2,
  "caption": "The worked example. Air enters the bed at 60 °C with Y = 0.010 and, while the solid is wet, cools along its adiabatic line as it climbs. Picking up 0.012 kg/kg it leaves at 31.5 °C and about 75 % RH, short of its limit, the adiabatic saturation point 27.6 °C with Y = 0.0236; that gap is ln 8.3 = 2.1 heat-transfer units of bed. 20 kg of water at 6 kg/h takes 3.3 h.",
