 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A through-circulation tray dryer handles 100 kg of dry solid, to be dried from X1 = 0.25 to X2 = 0.05 kg water/kg dry solid. Air enters the bed at 60 °C with Y1 = 0.010 at 500 kg dry air/h and leaves at Y2 = 0.022. Find the drying time. Check that the outlet air is not saturated.",
   "steps": [
    "Dry solid mass M<sub>s</sub> = 100 kg. Initial moisture X1 = 0.25 kg water/kg dry solid. Final moisture X2 = 0.05 kg water/kg dry solid.",
    "Water to remove = M<sub>s</sub> (X1 − X2) = 100 × (0.25 − 0.05) = 20 kg.",
    "Dry air flow m<sub>da</sub> = 500 kg dry air/h. Inlet humidity Y1 = 0.010 kg water/kg dry air. Outlet humidity Y2 = 0.022 kg water/kg dry air.",
    "Water picked up by air = m<sub>da</sub> (Y2 − Y1) = 500 × (0.022 − 0.010) = 6 kg/h.",
    "Drying time t = water to remove / water pickup = 20 kg / 6 kg/h = 3.3 h.",
    "Check outlet: at about 30 °C the saturation humidity is near 0.027 kg/kg, above 0.022, so the air leaves unsaturated and will not condense water back onto the solid."
   ],
   "answer": "The drying time is about 3.3 h.",
   "check": "Ms = 100.0\nX1 = 0.25\nX2 = 0.05\nmda = 500.0\nY1 = 0.010\nY2 = 0.022\nwater = Ms * (X1 - X2)\nrate = mda * (Y2 - Y1)\nt = water / rate\nprint(f\"{water:.1f}\")\nprint(f\"{rate:.1f}\")\nprint(f\"{t:.1f}\")\n",
   "values": [
    20.0,
    6.0,
    3.3
   ],
   "output": ""
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Putting tunnel dryers in the batch list: a tunnel dryer is continuous because trucks move through it without stopping.",
  "Confusing through-circulation with cross-circulation: through means air passes through the bed; cross means air passes over the tray surface.",
  "Using wet-basis moisture in place of dry-basis X: the formula uses kg water per kg dry solid.",
  "Forgetting the saturation limit: if outlet air would be above saturation, the computed Y2 is impossible and the time is longer.",
  "Mixing units: m<sub>da</sub> in kg dry air/h and t in h; if m<sub>da</sub> is in kg/s, t comes in seconds."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Name three batch dryer types.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "Tray or compartment, vacuum shelf, and freeze dryers."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does air temperature fall as it passes through a through-circulation bed?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Evaporation takes latent heat from the air, so sensible heat converts to water vapour and the air cools."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A dryer removes 30 kg of water in 3 h. What is the average drying rate?",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "Rate = 30 kg / 3 h = 10 kg/h."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Air enters a bed at Y = 0.012 and leaves at Y = 0.032 at 400 kg dry air/h. Find the water pickup and say whether the bed is drying.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Pickup = 400 × (0.032 − 0.012) = 8 kg/h. Yes, water is leaving the solid and entering the air."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "100 kg dry solid goes from X = 0.20 to X = 0.04 kg/kg dry solid. Air pickup is 12 kg/h. Find the drying time.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Water to remove = 100 × (0.20 − 0.04) = 16 kg. Time = 16 / 12 = 1.33 h."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why not use a spray dryer for large pottery slabs?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "A spray dryer needs an atomisable slurry. Slabs cannot be atomised; tray or tunnel dryers suit them better."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. Industrial dryers are now selected with energy audits, because drying can be the largest steam load in a plant. Heat-pump dryers recover latent heat; solar dryers suit rural food processing. Research tracks moisture profiles inside particles using MRI and X-ray tomography. Search for 'through-circulation drying pressure drop' and 'dryer selection guide'."
}